      "explanation": "B. The starting point favours the seller: the lawyer is not a party to the agreement and so appears not to be obliged to honour the direction if the seller directs otherwise before closing or there are prior claims to the proceeds. But the courts have held that where the seller executed under seal, the irrevocable instruction is binding on the lawyer and non-compliance results in liability. It is also arguable that the lawyer holding the proceeds is a trustee for the broker as well as for the seller. A states the general position and stops one sentence too early, which is the trap. C ignores the seal, which is the operative fact. D invents a procedure. The practical answer where the demands conflict is the one the material gives: be careful about refusing to pay the balance of commission simply because the seller has revoked the irrevocable direction, and consider paying the disputed funds into court under R. 43 of the Rules of Civil Procedure, or in some cases under s. 36 of the Trustee Act.",
