      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "B. Where the mortgage confers a power of sale, the mortgagee proceeds under the mortgage and Part III of the Mortgages Act. Where it does not, Part II of the Mortgages Act supplies a statutory power of sale. Either way, read the mortgage first. A and C treat the contractual power as indispensable. D invents a leave requirement; power of sale is an out-of-court remedy, which is exactly why mortgagees prefer it to judicial sale and foreclosure. Hold the related timing rule with it, from s. 32: notice of exercise of the power of sale cannot be given until the default has continued for at least 15 days, and the sale cannot be made for at least 35 days after notice. And note the different track for non-payment defaults: for a breach of covenant other than payment of principal or interest, such as failure to pay taxes or insurance premiums, the mortgagee must give the mortgagor notice of the breach and a reasonable time to remedy it before issuing a notice of sale.",
