      "explanation": "B. Prior encumbrancers need not be served, for a reason that explains the whole scheme: a power of sale does not extinguish the rights of those who rank ahead of the selling mortgagee. Their interests survive the sale, so the notice is nothing to them. A, C and D must all be served. Subsequent mortgagees are served because the sale will wipe out their security. Spouses are served because ss. 19(1) and 22(1) and (2) of the Family Law Act give the mortgagor's spouse rights of possession and redemption paralleling the titleholder's, which is why notice should go to the spouse of every named individual owner. Execution creditors are served under ss. 31(1)1 and 31(1)2 if their writs are subsequent to the mortgage and in the sheriff's hands, or filed in the land titles office, at the time the notice is given. Two more from the same list. The current owner of the equity of redemption is entitled to notice. The original mortgagor need not be served if the property has been sold, but must be if the mortgagee wants to sue on the covenant to pay, because failing to serve may relieve the original mortgagor of liability for the deficiency.",
